<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	Privacy manifest. Required at submission since spring 2024.

	Kairos collects nothing, and that is not a marketing claim — it has no
	networking code at all. The shared secrets live in the Keychain with
	kSecAttrAccessibleWhenUnlockedThisDeviceOnly, so they are not in an
	iCloud backup and do not follow the user to a new device. Codes are
	computed locally from the clock.

	NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes is empty because Kairos touches none of the
	required-reason categories: Keychain is not one of them, and it stores
	accounts there rather than in UserDefaults precisely because secrets do
	not belong in a plist.

	The camera on the iPhone side is a permission, not a collection: the QR
	code is decoded in memory and never stored or transmitted. Its purpose
	string is NSCameraUsageDescription in PhoneApp/Info.plist.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array/>
</dict>
</plist>
